  "body": "After a loop cut (cutLoop), the error magpie appends to a Chat stream that already went out comes after a chunk of its own: `streamError` makes a new chatEncoder, and its `event` starts a message before the error, with magpie's own id and an empty model.\n\nOn 68ee4117, the chat case of TestLoopEndsTheReplyOnEveryProtocol, as the agent reads it (consecutive chunks with the same id and model counted once):\n\n```\nid \"c1\", model \"deepseek-v4-max\"                      x710, the vendor's chunks\nid \"chatcmpl-18e227bb3e667b6700000001\", model \"\"      x1\n{\"error\":{\"message\":\"magpie ended this reply: the model's reasoning was stuck in a loop, ...\"}}\n```\n\nfx 0.0.14 checks each chunk's id and then its model against the first chunk's (`accept_identity` in chat_completions_protocol.zig) right after it looks for an `error` key, so it stops at magpie's chunk with ConflictingIdentity and never reads why the reply ended.\n\n#3728 is the same start when nothing went before the error but keepalives; its patch names the model there and leaves this case alone. Here a model in the chunk wouldn't help, the id still changes: the error belongs to the message already going, so it shouldn't start one. The passthrough's own mid-reply error (`streamFailure`) is the event alone, which looks like the right shape here, though the loop tests pin streamError's.\n\nPossibly the same: a Chat reasoning loop asked again (`mayReloop`) lets the second stream's chunks follow the first's, and a real vendor gives the second its own id. Not tried with fx, the fake vendor in the test uses \"c1\" both times.\n",
